Think of Spark’s architecture like a massive construction project.
You have the Driver (the general contractor) who reads the blueprint and assigns the work,
the Cluster Manager (the resource agency) who provides the raw labor and machinery,
and the Executors (the workers) who actually swing the hammers and do the heavy lifting.

Technical Breakdown :-

The Driver (The Brain): This is where your main() method runs. It takes your PySpark or SQL code, translates it into a logical Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG),
and breaks that DAG down into physical, distributable 'Tasks'.

The Cluster Manager (The Resource Negotiator): The Driver can't just spin up machines itself.
It asks the Cluster Manager—like YARN, Kubernetes, or Spark Standalone—for resources.
The Cluster Manager allocates the necessary RAM and CPU cores across the worker nodes.

The Executors (The Brawn): Once resources are granted, the Driver sends the Tasks directly to the Executors.
Executors live on the worker nodes, crunch the data, store intermediate results in their local memory (RAM), and eventually report the final status
or return data back to the Driver.

Buisness Context :- 

This decoupled architecture gives businesses two massive advantages: extreme horizontal scalability and fault tolerance.
If an Executor node suddenly dies due to a hardware failure, the job doesn't fail.
The Driver simply detects the lost heartbeat, requests a new Executor from the Cluster Manager, and re-assigns the lost tasks seamlessly.

Design Decision :-

Historically, everyone ran Spark on top of Hadoop YARN. But as a modern data engineer,
I am pushing for Kubernetes (K8s) as the cluster manager.
By containerizing Spark jobs with Docker and letting K8s manage the resources, we stop paying for idle monolithic Hadoop clusters.
We can spin up ephemeral, perfectly isolated Spark clusters on demand, and tear them down the second the pipeline finishes, radically optimizing our cloud compute costs.
